+\subsection{DAG-Based Agent Workflow Serving} \label{sec:background:serving} 
 
+A multi-agent application coordinates multiple role-specialized agents to complete a user task. An agent may generate intermediate results, invoke external tools, delegate subtasks, and consume outputs produced by other agents. These dependencies form a directed acyclic graph (DAG), in which each node represents an agent invocation and each edge represents a data or control dependency. 
 
+A \textbf{workflow} runtime maintains the DAG and releases an invocation after its dependencies have been satisfied. Forks allow multiple invocations to execute in parallel, while joins delay downstream invocations until all required predecessors have completed~\cite{lin2024parrot}. 
+%
+An \textbf{agent} can be invoked multiple times during one workflow. On each {turn}, its input may include an agent-specific instruction, previous model outputs, tool results, and outputs received from other agents. These inputs form an evolving agent history retained by the workflow runtime. 
 
-\paragraph{Workflow completion time.}
-Let $w$ represent a workflow, $a_w$ the time the workflow arrives at the system, and $c_w$ the time when all necessary outputs are ready. The total time from arrival to completion of the workflow is defined as the \emph{Workflow Completion Time} (WCT), i.e., $\mathrm{WCT}_w=c_w-a_w$. It includes model execution, tool execution, queuing, and dependency waiting. In the code example, integration needs to wait for all necessary modifications. Delays on the last incomplete branch will delay integration, while branches that are completed earlier may absorb the same delay before merging. Request delays affect the WCT through these dependencies.
+Figure~\ref{fig:serving_model} shows how agent invocations are mapped to an LLM serving system. Ready invocations are submitted as inference requests and scheduled across model workers. A \textbf{worker} contains one model replica, or a distributed replica spanning a tensor-parallel~\cite{shoeybi2019megatron} group, and may concurrently serve agents from multiple workflows. 
+%
+Processing a request consists of prefill and decoding. During prefill, the model processes the input tokens and generates a key-value (KV) cache for each transformer layer. During decoding, newly generated tokens append entries to this cache. When a later invocation reuses a prefix of the same agent history, the corresponding KV entries allow the worker to process only the newly appended input rather than prefill the complete history again~\cite{gao2024cachedattention,zheng2024sglang}. Consequently, an active agent accumulates a KV history across its iterative turns. 
 
